1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:05,184
It is Sunday morning, and Aziz opens the mobile bank to pay for his gym.

2
00:00:05,234 --> 00:00:11,544
A yellow line sits at the top of the screen: your passport expires soon, please refresh your identity.

3
00:00:11,594 --> 00:00:16,728
His face has not changed, his phone has not changed, and his password has not changed.

4
00:00:16,778 --> 00:00:20,160
So why does the bank care about a date in a small book in his drawer?

5
00:00:20,210 --> 00:00:30,264
In Lesson 15, MyID proved Aziz with a full proofing, and in Lesson 17 the platform kept the result as a verified identity, in versions.

6
00:00:30,314 --> 00:00:38,256
This lesson adds time: a document ends, and the record must follow it, without one field changing behind Aziz's back.

7
00:00:38,306 --> 00:00:40,128
Start with the date.

8
00:00:40,178 --> 00:00:44,184
The verified identity keeps the expiry date of the document.

9
00:00:44,234 --> 00:00:50,544
From that date, the platform calculates a document status: valid, expiring or expired.

10
00:00:50,594 --> 00:00:56,424
Expiring starts the configured lead time before the date, and expired starts after the date.

11
00:00:56,474 --> 00:01:02,640
A job in the workers calculates the status again one time each day, and again at each refresh.

12
00:01:02,690 --> 00:01:08,736
When the status becomes expiring, the platform writes one audit event: document expiring.

13
00:01:08,786 --> 00:01:13,488
From then on, the bank's app asks Aziz to do a refresh at each start.

14
00:01:13,538 --> 00:01:15,888
That is the yellow line he sees on Sunday.

15
00:01:15,938 --> 00:01:20,280
Aziz is busy, so he taps later, and the date passes.

16
00:01:20,330 --> 00:01:22,296
Now the status is expired.

17
00:01:22,346 --> 00:01:26,520
What happens next is the part I like most, because of what does not happen.

18
00:01:26,570 --> 00:01:28,632
The access grant stays valid.

19
00:01:28,682 --> 00:01:33,024
The platform does not block Aziz, and it does not ask for MyID at login.

20
00:01:33,074 --> 00:01:40,680
Instead, the identity summary that the app reads gets a restricted flag, and a list of allowed actions that the bank configures.

21
00:01:40,730 --> 00:01:48,240
Aziz gets the security notification document expired, by SMS to his verified number and by push to his devices.

22
00:01:48,290 --> 00:01:51,360
The trail gets the audit event document expired.

23
00:01:51,410 --> 00:01:59,064
In this first version, the app of the bank applies the restriction from the flag, and a later phase moves that rule into the platform.

24
00:01:59,114 --> 00:02:02,976
Only one thing removes the flag: a successful refresh.

25
00:02:03,026 --> 00:02:04,848
So what is a refresh?

26
00:02:04,898 --> 00:02:08,352
It is not a form where someone types the new passport number.

27
00:02:08,402 --> 00:02:12,072
It is a new full proofing, with the purpose refresh.

28
00:02:12,122 --> 00:02:14,640
A refresh can start in three ways.

29
00:02:14,690 --> 00:02:19,440
Aziz can start it himself, for example on the day he gets his new passport.

30
00:02:19,490 --> 00:02:23,280
The app starts it while the status is expiring or expired.

31
00:02:23,330 --> 00:02:28,560
And an operator can ask for it, with a reason, and the app shows that request at the next start.

32
00:02:28,610 --> 00:02:33,624
Before it begins, a refresh needs a trusted device and a valid access grant.

33
00:02:33,674 --> 00:02:35,784
Then it takes the road from module four.

34
00:02:35,834 --> 00:02:39,888
The consent gate shows its text, and Aziz accepts it.

35
00:02:39,938 --> 00:02:47,736
The platform opens a MyID session with his personal identification number already set, so the master app goes straight to the face.

36
00:02:47,786 --> 00:02:52,392
Aziz looks into the camera, and MyID compares his face with the state records.

37
00:02:52,442 --> 00:02:56,544
The result comes back, and Aziz confirms the data on his screen.

38
00:02:56,594 --> 00:03:01,176
One step is missing on purpose: a refresh does not do the phone check.

39
00:03:01,226 --> 00:03:03,048
Now the result.

40
00:03:03,098 --> 00:03:10,512
The personal identification number is the same, so the platform writes a new version of the verified identity and makes it current.

41
00:03:10,562 --> 00:03:12,384
It keeps the earlier version.

42
00:03:12,434 --> 00:03:18,168
The reusable identifier and its expiry date are new too, so later re-checks can use them.

43
00:03:18,218 --> 00:03:23,880
The document status is calculated again: valid, and the restricted flag goes away.

44
00:03:23,930 --> 00:03:31,152
Aziz gets the security notification identity refreshed, and the trail gets the audit event identity refreshed.

45
00:03:31,202 --> 00:03:37,152
Here is the rule under all of this, and I think it is the most important sentence of the lesson.

46
00:03:37,202 --> 00:03:45,216
No field of the verified identity changes without a live face check that the customer completed, in a session that the platform opened.

47
00:03:45,266 --> 00:03:51,096
Not a support ticket, not an import from another system, not an operator with good intentions.

48
00:03:51,146 --> 00:03:56,160
An operator can ask for a refresh, but an operator cannot type a new surname.

49
00:03:56,210 --> 00:04:02,928
A refresh also obeys the attempt limits of Lesson 11, for the personal identification number and the device.

50
00:04:02,978 --> 00:04:06,456
Only a refresh that an operator asks for is not counted.

51
00:04:06,506 --> 00:04:11,688
To recap: the expiry date gives a status, and the status gives a reaction.

52
00:04:11,738 --> 00:04:16,200
Expiring asks, expired restricts, and neither one blocks.

53
00:04:16,250 --> 00:04:21,600
Only a new live face check changes the record, as a new version next to the old one.

54
00:04:21,650 --> 00:04:28,944
But there is one case where a refresh does end in a block: when the result carries a different personal identification number.

55
00:04:28,994 --> 00:04:32,400
So what does a block really do, and who is allowed to lift it?

56
00:04:32,450 --> 00:04:33,792
Next: blocks.
